   131	# `verdict` (it strips the key), while the local tag guard's regex demanded
   132	# the colon immediately after the name and skipped the line entirely. The
   133	# valid-YAML pair
   134	#
   135	#     verdict : NO-GO
   136	#     verdict: GO
   137	#
   138	# is therefore two declarations here and ONE (=GO) there — a last-wins
   139	# override that the rail enforcing in every mode never saw.
   140	#
   141	# The semantics chosen for BOTH rails, and the reason: NON-CANONICAL
   142	# top-level syntax is REJECTED, fail-closed. Trimming the key in both
   143	# readers would also close the divergence, but it widens the grammar of a
   144	# SIGNED artifact to shapes no author writes and no template emits — every
   145	# new accepted spelling is another chance for the next reader to disagree.
   146	# Refusing the shape is a smaller surface and is the one behaviour that
   147	# cannot silently prefer a value. A top-level line is canonical iff it is
   148	# `name:` with no whitespace before the colon; list items are legal
   149	# ONLY indented under an active bare key (t8 P2: a root `- item` is
   150	# rejected — the old comment said otherwise and misled operators).
